   194	## 4. `NO-DEG-CAP`: theorem, proof, and exact scope
   195	
   196	There is a proof here, but of a narrower conditional statement.  Let
   197	`K_N` be the hypothetical RED-N counterexample class.  The safe formulation is
   198	
   199	```text
   200	for every F in K_N and every B, some T in Aut(C^2) has deg A_(T o F)>B.
   201	```
   202	
   203	RN:337-340 omits one needed sentence.  Choose a linear target coordinate `x`
   204	nonconstant on at least one component (a component on which `x` is constant is a
   205	line, already excluded by NL).  For a normalization parametrization
   206	`(x(t),y(t))`, the shear `T_k(x,y)=(x,y+x^k)` has second-coordinate pole order
   207	`k deg x(t)` for all sufficiently large `k`, with no cancellation against the
   208	fixed `y(t)`.  Hence that component, and therefore `A_(T_k o F)`, has unbounded
   209	raw degree.  This repairs the orbit lemma.
   210	
   211	It follows that
   212	
   213	```text
   214	K_N nonempty  =>  sup{deg A_F : F in K_N}=infinity,
   215	```
   216	
   217	or equivalently that any universal raw-coordinate cap would imply `K_N` empty.
   218	It does **not** prove that no cap theorem exists: if the Keller counterexample
   219	class is empty, every cap is vacuously true.  Thus RN:342-344 and 547-549 commit a
   220	quantifier error by declaring N5 DQ-3 refuted/closed negative.  A proved cap would
   221	still close that lane--indeed all counterexample lanes--by contradiction.  The
   222	useful campaign conclusion is instead that a nonvacuous within-cage degree bound
   223	must be stated in an automorphism-normalized gauge such as `d_min`.
   224	
   225	The claims at RN:346-356 go further and are false.  A target automorphism is an
   226	isomorphism of affine curves: it preserves normalization, affine singular points
   227	and their analytic types; geometric genus remains zero.  It changes projective
   228	degree and the singularity data at infinity, which compensate in the genus
   229	formula.  Raw-degree unboundedness therefore does not imply that “genera and
   230	singularity counts are all unbounded,” nor that no inequality using
   231	degree/genus/delta data can close a class.  The common-`eta` compatibility gate in
   232	SS2 is already a finite counterexample to the alleged exhaustion of the numerical
   233	layer.  `NO-DEG-CAP` is a proved conditional gauge observation, not an
   234	impossibility theorem about future gates or survivor classifications.
   235	
   236	## 5. Survivor-shape and class decomposition
   237	
   238	RN's “core plus trivial distribution” is a useful **one-way necessary
   239	parametrization**, after adding the common-`eta` rule.  It is not a classification
   240	by the displayed class label.  Four independent typing failures prevent one OPEN
   241	question per `[lambda_i^(k_i)]` from deciding the carrier profiles.
   242	
   243	First, `lambda_i` records only moved cycles.  If `r` trivial carriers lie on its
   244	component, `a_i=N-W_i` counts affine fixed sheets but omits the `r` fixed boundary
   245	clusters.  The meridian's permutation type is
   246	
   247	```text
   248	lambda_i * 1^(N-|lambda_i|),
   249	```
   250	
   251	not `lambda_i * 1^(a_i)` as RN:503-504 states.  As printed, some permutations do
   252	not even have degree `N`; this also explains why placements with different `a_i`
   253	cannot be encoded by that OPEN.
   254	
   255	Second, the cusp clause RN:499-502 applies the `mu=2` calculation to every
   256	correction.  `[3^(1)]` at `N=6` is already a counterexample to the scope; the
   257	`mu=3,4` correction germs in the `N=7,8` rows are explicitly left OPEN by
   258	RN:560-563.  A negative answer to RN's artificially narrow ordinary-cusp question
   259	would not kill the true `mu>=3` class.
   260	
   261	Third, component-total `K_i` is not a physical singularity weight.  If two
   262	degree-one carriers own the same component, they see the same critical
   263	normalization places.  Even granting RN's global `mu=2` cusp dictionary for an
   264	internal consistency test, two `(2,1,1)` carriers in `[22^(2)]` see the **same**
   265	ordinary cusp of curve weight one; summing carrier corrections gives `K_i=2`.
   266	RN's demand `sum(nu-1)=k_i` double-charges one physical place.  Without the
   267	unproved global dictionary the germ is simply OPEN, but the carrier/place
   268	conflation remains.
   269	
   270	Fourth, the abstract curve question omits conditions asserted by CAGE-N itself:
   271	the common Newton--Puiseux exponent pair and degree divisibility, exclusion of a
   272	smoothly embedded `A^1` (not merely a literal line), TRANS-LOC, the unbranched
   273	component incidence needed to state LOC, and carrier-to-cluster decorations.
   274	Its TG variables `g` and `Sigma_inf` are not defined from the abstract
   275	representation.  A positive answer is only a broad topological possibility, not
   276	a profile witness; for corrected `mu>=3` rows even a negative answer is aimed at
   277	the wrong hypothesis class.
   278	
   279	The safe successor is therefore
   280	
   281	```text
   282	OPEN[PI1-S_N-CAGE(carriers, owners, places)]
   283	```
   284	
   285	with every `(mu,s,K)` retained; degree-one carriers coupled through their common
   286	normalization map; ramification points separated from contracted attachments;
   287	each physical singular branch listed once; `mu>=3` germs unrestricted and typed
   288	OPEN; total fixed exponent `N-|lambda|`; and unbranched owners retained for LOC
   289	even though their meridians die in the global representation.  Several such
   290	decorated questions may project to the same moved-cycle label.  Consequently the
   291	claimed general survivor-shape classification and the `6/10/26` per-class OPEN
   292	package are not promotable.
