   114	### 2.2 CUSP-2 at the scope Orevkov Lemma 5.2 licenses
   115	
   116	Orevkov Lemma 5.2 `[L]` (printed p. 8): *let `x` in `X~^*`, `x != inf`, lie on the
   117	image `l^* = pi(l)` of an irreducible component `l` of `L_F`; **if
   118	`mu_x f^* = mu_l f^*`** then the germ of `f^*|_{l^*}` at `x` determines a
   119	**nonsingular** branch of `f^*(l^*)`.* The implication runs
   120	`equality => nonsingular`, contrapositive `singular => M_t > mu_l`. R1's NO-RAM-2
   121	used the **converse**, which the source does not supply. GATE is right.
   122	`[A]` **AUDIT-R2-1.**
   123	
   124	The proof of Lemma 5.2 settles the *other* half of GATE's objection and sharpens
   125	it. Orevkov writes "*It is clear that if `x` is nonsingular in `X^*` (i.e. `x` not
   126	in `pi(L_C)`) then `H ~ Z`. It turns out ... that if `x` is singular then also
   127	`H ~ Z`*", invoking his Lemma 2.1 for the fact that `pi^{-1}(x)` is a **linear
   128	chain** `l_1, ..., l_{k-1}` of contracted curves which the dicritical `l = l_k`
   129	meets transversally in `l_{k-1}`. So Lemma 5.2 is itself proved at the
   130	quotient-singular points, and nothing in R1's use of *that* lemma fails for being
   131	at an attachment. But the geometry it exhibits is exactly what CUSP-2 excludes
   132	without saying so. At `t in l n l_{k-1}` the source surface `X~` **is** smooth -
   133	GATE's "after contraction there is no smooth two-coordinate source germ"
   134	overstates it - yet a *second* boundary divisor `l_{k-1}` passes through `t` and
   135	is contracted by `f~`, hence lies in the ramification divisor. R1's step
   136	"`J = y^{mu_l-1} h` with `h` a unit, since a zero of `h` would force a curve of
   137	zeros of `J` off `l`" is then false: `l_{k-1}` is such a curve.
   138	
   139	> **Lemma CUSP-2R `[D]`+`[L]`.** Let `mu_l = 2` and let `t in l'` be a point with
   140	> `alpha := ord_t(u_0) >= 2` (after the linear target change making
   141	> `alpha < beta`) at which **no boundary divisor other than `l` passes**, i.e.
   142	> `pi(t)` is a smooth point of `X~^*`. Then `beta = alpha + 1`, `u_1(0) != 0`,
   143	> `M_t = alpha + 1`, `e_t = 1`, the image branch has multiplicity `nu = alpha`,
   144	> its unique Puiseux pair is `(nu, nu+1)`, and `k_t = nu - 1`.
   145	
   146	The computation is R1's, re-checked line by line; two things move. The hypothesis
   147	"the branch at `phi_l(t)` is singular" is replaced by `alpha >= 2`, which is all
   148	the proof ever used ("*singularity of the branch means `alpha >= 2`*"), removing
   149	the appeal to the converse of Lemma 5.2 entirely; and `J = y h` with `h` a unit is
   150	now a *hypothesis*, licensed at `t` off the contracted locus and off `L_inf`.
   151	
   152	> **Theorem NO-RAM-2R `[D]`.** Let `mu_l = 2`. If every ramification point of
   153	> `h_l` (every `t in l'` with `e_t >= 2`) lies off the contracted locus, then
   154	> `s_l = 1`.
   155	
   156	*Proof.* If `s_l >= 2`, Lemma A gives `t` with `e_t >= 2`. Then
   157	`ord_t(phi_l - phi_l(t)) = e_t . nu >= 2`, so `alpha >= 2` and CUSP-2R applies at
   158	`t`, giving `beta = alpha + 1`; but `e_t | (beta - alpha) = 1`. Contradiction. []
   159	
   160	So a `mu = 2` carrier with `s_l >= 2` must hide **all** its `h_l`-ramification at
   161	attachment points. The cusp dictionary (`corr_l = K_l = sum (nu-1)`, each
   162	correction an ordinary `(nu, nu+1)` germ) and the ramification threshold inherit
   163	that hypothesis:
   164	
   165	- the dictionary holds off the contracted locus and is silent at attachment sites;
