   241	### 4.1 Pole orders and the leading-form equation
   242	
   243	For a candidate pair put `alpha = -v(f)`, `beta = -v(g)`. Proposition 2.3 gives
   244	`alpha, beta <= 3` in the `A`-degree-2 class, and `f_{-3}`, `g_{-3}` odd.
   245	Applying (2.3) at `m=-alpha-beta`, where only `(i,j)=(-alpha,-beta)` occurs,
   246	
   247	```text
   248	alpha * f_{-alpha} * g_{-beta}' = beta * f_{-alpha}' * g_{-beta}.      (N0)
   249	```
   250	
   251	Equivalently `d/dV log( g_{-beta}^alpha / f_{-alpha}^beta ) = 0`, so with
   252	`k0=gcd(alpha,beta)` there is `h in C[V]` and nonzero constants with
   253	
   254	```text
   255	f_{-alpha} = c_1 h^{alpha/k0},    g_{-beta} = c_2 h^{beta/k0}.         (4.1)
   256	```
   257	
   258	This is the second-order invariant asked for: it is the discriminant-free
   259	integrated form of the leading system, and unlike the bottom-of-tower equations
   260	of Section 3 it admits **no** compensator, because at `A`-degree 2 there is no
   261	level-3 datum to absorb it.
   262	
   263	**Parity transfer.** If `u^k` is `sigma`-odd for odd `k`, then `u` is odd:
   264	`sigma(u)^k=(-u)^k` forces `sigma(u)=-zeta u` with `zeta^k=1`, and applying
   265	`sigma` again gives `zeta^2=1`, hence `zeta=1`. So whenever `alpha=3` in (4.1),
   266	`h` is an odd polynomial, `h(0)=0`, `deg h >= 1`.
