   275	If `ph'=0` then `f` is constant and `{f,g}=0`. So `ph' != 0` and `(R_0)_A=0`,
   276	i.e. `R_0=r(Z)`. Then `E = -ph' [ 4H (S_0)_A + c S_0 ]`. Since a product of two
   277	elements of `C[A,Z]` equals the nonzero constant `kappa`, both factors are
   278	nonzero constants. Writing `S_0=sum_n L_n A^n`,
   279	
   280	```text
   281	4H (S_0)_A + c S_0 = sum_n [ (4n+2) L_n + 4n Z L_{n-1} ] A^n,
   282	```
   283	
   284	so `L_0 = -kappa/(2 ph') != 0` and `L_n = -(2nZ/(2n+1)) L_{n-1}` for `n >= 1`.
   285	By induction `L_n != 0` for every `n`, contradicting `S_0 in C[A,Z]`. QED
   286	
   287	Lemma 4.1 is genuinely stronger than `theta:(3.2)` in one direction (it puts no
   288	cap on `g`) and weaker in another (it needs `Q_f=0`); the two are complementary
   289	and both are used below. Combining with (2.4): `v(f) >= 0` is impossible, so
   290	
   291	```text
   292	alpha >= 1  and  beta >= 1  for every solution, at any A-degree.       (4.2)
   293	```
   294	
   295	### 4.3 Affine normal form and the census
   296	
   297	The target group `Aff_2(C)` acts by `(f,g) -> (af+bg+e, cf+dg+e')`, replacing
   298	`kappa` by `(ad-bc)kappa != 0`. Each of `P,Q,R_0,S_0` transforms `C`-linearly,
   299	so the `A`-degree-2 class is `Aff_2`-stable, and proving
   300	`OBSTRUCTION[A-DEGREE-TWO]` for normalized pairs proves it outright. No residue
   301	normalization is used or needed.
   302	
   303	**Proposition 4.2 (normal form).** Any `A`-degree-2 solution can be moved by
   304	`Aff_2` to one with `alpha > beta >= 1`, hence with
   305	
   306	```text
   307	(alpha,beta) in { (3,2), (3,1), (2,1) }.                              (4.3)
   308	```
   309	
   310	*Proof.* Swap `f,g` so `alpha >= beta`. If `alpha = beta` then (N0) reads
   311	`Wr(f_{-alpha}, g_{-beta}) = 0`, so `g_{-beta} = lambda f_{-alpha}` for a
   312	nonzero constant and `g - lambda f` has strictly smaller pole order while
   313	staying in the class; one such step suffices. Then (4.2) gives `beta >= 1` and
   314	Proposition 2.3 gives `alpha <= 3`. QED
   315	
   316	Three cells. Sections 5 and 6 dispose of two and reduce the third to an
   317	explicit rigid system. Note that (4.3) already forbids `alpha = beta`, which is
   318	the classical "same degree" case; the surviving arithmetic is
   319	`(alpha,beta)=(3,2)` with `gcd = 1`.
   320	
   321	## 5. Cells (3,1) and (2,1) are empty
   322	
   323	Throughout, `beta=1` forces, by Proposition 2.3 read coefficientwise,
   324	
   325	```text
   326	g_{-3}=V L_2(V^2) = 0  =>  L_2=0;
   327	g_{-2}=E_2(V^2)+V L_1(V^2) = 0  =>  E_2=L_1=0;
   328	g_{-1}=E_1(V^2)+V L_0(V^2) != 0.                                       (5.1)
   329	```
   330	
   331	### 5.1 Cell (3,1)
   332	
   333	Here `alpha=3`, so `f_{-3}` is odd and nonzero, and (4.1) with `k0=1` gives
   334	`f_{-3}=c' k^3` where `k := g_{-1}`. By the parity transfer of §4.1, `k` is
   335	odd, so by (5.1) `E_1=0`. Hence
   336	
   337	```text
   338	R_0=r(Z),  S_0=s(Z),   i.e. g has A-degree 0, and k = V s(V^2).       (5.2)
   339	```
   340	
   341	Consequently `g^2=(r^2+Hs^2)+U(2rs)` and `g^3=(r^3+3Hrs^2)+U(3r^2s+Hs^3)`
   342	both lie in the `A`-degree-2 class, because `deg_A H = 2`. Now read (2.3) at
   343	`m=-3`, where only `(i,j)=(-3,0)` and `(-2,-1)` occur:
   344	
   345	```text
   346	3 f_{-3} g_0' - f_{-2}' g_{-1} + 2 f_{-2} g_{-1}' = 0.
   347	```
   348	
   349	Since `f_{-2}' k - 2 f_{-2} k' = k^3 (f_{-2} k^{-2})'`, this integrates once:
   350	
   351	```text
   352	f_{-2} = k^2 ( 3c' g_0 + c_3 ),      c_3 in C.                        (5.3)
   353	```
   354	
   355	But `(g^3)_{-3}=k^3`, `(g^3)_{-2}=3k^2 g_0` and `(g^2)_{-2}=k^2`, so (5.3) says
   356	exactly that
   357	
   358	```text
   359	ft := f - c' g^3 - c_3 g^2   satisfies  v(ft) >= -1,
   360	```
   361	
   362	while `ft` is still in the `A`-degree-2 class and `{ft,g}={f,g}=kappa`. If
   363	`v(ft)=-1` then `alpha=beta=1` and one more `Aff_2` step (Proposition 4.2)
   364	gives `fh` with `v(fh)>=0`; by (2.4), `fh in C[Z]`. Lemma 4.1 now contradicts
   365	`{fh,g}=kappa`. Cell `(3,1)` is empty.
   366	
   367	### 5.2 Cell (2,1)
   368	
   369	Here `alpha=2`, so `D_2=0`, `f_{-3}=0` and
   370	`f_{-2}=C_2(V^2)+V D_1(V^2) != 0`. Equation (4.1) with `(alpha,beta)=(2,1)`
   371	gives `f_{-2}=c_0 k^2`, `k=g_{-1}`. No parity is available here (a square
   372	carries none), so `E_1` need not vanish; but by (5.1)
   373	`R_0=r+A E_1`, `S_0=s`, whence
   374	
   375	```text
   376	g^2 = ( (r+A E_1)^2 + H s^2 ) + U ( 2 (r+A E_1) s ),
   377	```
   378	
   379	whose four coefficient polynomials have `A`-degrees `2` and `1`. So `g^2` is in
   380	the class, and `ft := f - c_0 g^2` has `v(ft) >= -1`, is in the class, and
   381	satisfies `{ft,g}=kappa`. Exactly as in §5.1, one further `Aff_2` step and
   382	(2.4) produce `fh in C[Z]` with `{fh,g}=kappa`, contradicting Lemma 4.1. Cell
   383	`(2,1)` is empty.
   384	
   385	### 5.3 What the two kills share
   386	
   387	Both use the same three-step pattern, which is the promotable technique of this
   388	report: (i) the leading-form equation (N0) identifies `f_{-alpha}` with a power
   389	of `g_{-beta}`; (ii) because `beta | alpha`, that power is realized by an actual
   390	element `g^{alpha/beta}` of `RR` which happens to stay inside the `A`-degree-2
   391	class -- this is where the truncation is exploited, and it is exactly the step
   392	Section 3 shows can never be done from the bottom; (iii) subtracting it is a
   393	*nonlinear* automorphism of the constraint (`{g^k,g}=0`), so the pole order
   394	drops without touching `kappa`, and the descent terminates on Lemma 4.1.
   395	
   396	Step (ii) is available precisely when `beta` divides `alpha`. This is why the
   397	census (4.3) has exactly one survivor.
