    49	## 2. Shared Tschirnhausen chart and conventions
    50	
    51	For `(8,6)` the frozen chart is
    52	
    53	```text
    54	p=t^8+B t^5+C t^4+D t^3+E t^2+F t+G,
    55	q=t^6+b t^4+gamma t^3+d t^2+e t+f,
    56	H=p^3-q^4=sum h_j t^j.
    57	```
    58	
    59	For `(9,6)` it is
    60	
    61	```text
    62	p=t^9+A t^7+P6 t^6+B t^5+P4 t^4+C t^3+P2 t^2+D t,
    63	q=t^6+a t^4+Q3 t^3+b t^2+Q1 t,
    64	K=p^2-q^3=sum k_j t^j.
    65	```
    66	
    67	These are exactly the charts and raw expansions at MSOLVE-PREP 2.1--2.2
    68	(lines 50--129).  The source/target gauges explain the displayed chart.
    69	In particular the absent `p:t^7,q:t^5` terms make `h_23=0`, and the absent
    70	`p:t^8,q:t^5` terms make `k_17=0`: these are the automatic first gap
    71	conditions, not extra generators.  None of these gauges turns a raw
    72	binomial remainder into the reduced second approximate root.
    73	
    74	Notation is kept disjoint throughout:
    75	
    76	- `c` is the third delta-sequence entry in `Delta=(d,n,c)`;
    77	- `beta_1` is the first transverse Puiseux numerator;
    78	- a *place* is a normalization point over the line at infinity;
    79	- the `a` Puiseux conjugate series (`a=2` or `3`) describe one physical
    80	  branch when the local equation is irreducible.  They are not `a` places.
