   118	vanishing of the `t^{16},\ldots,t^6` coefficients of `y^3-x^2` reduces to two equations in `(a,b,c)`. The slice `a=2` factors as `(b-1)^2(2b-5)=0`. The root `b=1` (with `c=0`) is the cusp `y^3=x^2`. The other root is `b=5/2`, `c=3/4`:
   119	
   120	```text
   121	x(t) = t^9 + 3 t^7 + (21/4) t^5 + (35/8) t^3 + (63/32) t
   122	y(t) = t^6 + 2 t^4 + (5/2) t^2 + 3/4
   123	```
   124	
   125	and
   126	
   127	```text
   128	y^3-x^2 = (27/1024)(8 t^4 + 13 t^2 + 16)
   129	```
   130	
   131	of degree exactly 4 (leading `t^4` coefficient `27/128≠0`). The parametrisation is proper: `gcd(9,6)=3` is the only possible reparametrisation degree, and a cubic reparametrisation would force `y^3=x^2`. Hence `C(x(t),y(t))=C(t)`, normalisation `A^1`, charged type.
   132	
   133	`r_0=deg_t x=9`, `r_1=deg_t y=6`, `r_2=deg_t(y^3-x^2)=4`, so the δ-sequence is `(9,6,4)`. The value semigroup contains `⟨4,6,9⟩`; with this sequence of length 3 it equals `⟨4,6,9⟩`, genus 6, therefore `Δ_{\mathrm{aff}}=6`.
   134	
   135	Derivatives:
   136	
   137	```text
   138	y' = t(6 t^4 + 8 t^2 + 5),     disc(6z^2+8z+5)=64-120<0
   139	x' = 9 t^8 + 21 t^6 + (105/4) t^4 + (105/8) t^2 + 63/32
   140	```
   141	
   142	At `t=0`, `x'(0)=63/32≠0`. Reducing `x'` along `6z^2+8z+5=0` gives `x'=(-27/8)z-117/32`, which vanishes only at the real value `z=-13/12`, not a root of `6z^2+8z+5`. Thus `x'=y'=0` has no solution: the parametrisation is an **immersion** (no affine cusps).
   143	
   144	All affine singularities are therefore multiple fibres. The involution `t↦-t` (`y` even, `x` odd) identifies `{t,-t}` whenever `x(t)=0`, `t≠0`. The even factor of `x(t)/t` is the quartic `q(z)=32z^4+96z^3+168z^2+140z+63`. Euclidean algorithm: `gcd(q,q')=1` (final remainder `15/4≠0`), so four distinct roots. Resultant of `q` against `6z^2+8z+5` is nonzero, so `y'≠0` at these fibres; `x'=2z q'(z)≠0` because the roots are simple. Velocities `(x',y')` and `(x',-y')` are linearly independent: four ordinary nodes (two smooth branches, distinct tangents), each of delta 1.
   145	
   146	These four points are distinct in the plane: if two roots `r,s` of `q` had `p(r)=p(s)` for `p(z)=z^3+2z^2+(5/2)z+3/4`, they would satisfy `Q(r,s)=r^2+rs+s^2+2r+2s+5/2=0`. The resulting cubic conditions in `σ=r+s` are `32σ^3+152σ^2+256σ+157=0` and `8σ^3+32σ^2+46σ+25=0`, whose gcd is 1. Four distinct ordinary nodes, affine delta 4, remaining affine delta `6-4=2`.
   147	
   148	Remaining delta 2 cannot be a three-branch germ (minimum delta 3 for three smooth branches). It cannot be unibranch (the parametrisation is an immersion). It is therefore a two-smooth-branch germ, hence of type `A_{2n-1}` with link `T(2,2n)`: either two further nodes, or one `A_3` (`n=2`). Both lie in BLZ 2024 Theorem 6.4. The split is not needed for licensing.
   149	
   150	```text
   151	charged record_aff:
   152	  four ordinary nodes (t ↔ -t on x=0), plus remaining δ=2 of type T(2,2n)
   153	  all affine germs two-smooth-branch; no unibranch affine point; no 3-branch point
   154	  Δ_aff = 6
   155	```
   156	
   157	This representative is not row-exhaustion and is not `FULL_ACTUAL_EXIT`. Other AG-S moduli may change the affine partition. It is the first explicit charged curve of the row `(9,6,4)`.
   158	
   159	## 4. Infinity analysis (this curve only)
   160	
   161	Work on the displayed polynomial curve of §3.2, not on a row-level Newton face.
   162	
   163	The map `P^1\to\overline{C}\subset P^2` sends `t=\infty` to a single point, so `(\overline{C},P_\infty)` is unibranch (one place at infinity is the definition of a polynomial curve). In the chart `X=1` at `[1:0:0]`, with `s=1/t`,
   164	
   165	```text
   166	Y = y/x = s^3 (1 - s^2 + (1/4) s^4 + (7/8) s^6 + O(s^8))
   167	Z = 1/x = s^9 (1 - 3 s^2 + (15/4) s^4 + (1/8) s^6 + O(s^8))
   168	```
   169	
   170	so `v(Y)=3` and `v(Z)=9\in⟨3⟩`. Homogenising a degree-9 equation, `p_a=28`; with `g_{\mathrm{geom}}=0` and `Δ_{\mathrm{aff}}=6` one has `δ_∞=22`. A unibranch germ with `v(Y)=3` and delta 22 has characteristic valuation `v(Z-Y^3)=23` and local semigroup `S_∞=⟨3,23⟩`: the coprime formula `(3-1)(23-1)/2=22` matches the budget, and the expansion already matches `Z` against `Y^3` through order `s^{17}`, consistent with a later first difference at order 23. Link `T(3,23)`. Conductor of `⟨3,23⟩` is `3\cdot 23-3-23=43`.
   171	
   172	```text
   173	record_infty (charged curve):
   174	  type             = UNIBRANCH_SEMIGROUP
   175	  place            = P_∞ of the degree-9 closure of §3.2

